    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, 'Which physical exam system is the standard classification used by ENT surgeons to predict the success of palatal surgery (UPPP) by grading tongue position and tonsil size?', '["The Cotton-Myer grading system", "The Friedman Staging System", "The Mallampati score", "The Cormack-Lehane system", "The House-Brackmann scale"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Friedman Staging System is the gold standard for UPPP candidate selection. It integrates Friedman tongue position (FTP), tonsil size, and BMI to categorize patients into Stages I, II, and III, correlating highly with surgical success.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'In the Friedman Staging System, a patient with Friedman Tongue Position (FTP) I, Grade 3 or 4 tonsils, and a BMI less than 40 is placed in which Stage, and what is their predicted UPPP success rate?', '["Stage I; ~80 percent success rate", "Stage III; ~10 percent success rate", "Stage II; ~40 percent success rate", "Stage IV; 0 percent success rate", "Stage I; ~10 percent success rate"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Stage I represents the ideal surgical candidate: small tongue/low palate position (FTP I), large tonsils (Grade 3/4), and a reasonable BMI. UPPP alone in these patients has an exceptionally high success rate (~80%).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with a Friedman Tongue Position (FTP) III (where the palate is not visible, only the hard palate is seen) and Grade 1 tonsils is categorized as Friedman Stage III. What is the expected success rate of a standard UPPP in this patient?', '["Over 90 percent", "Approximately 60 percent", "Less than 10-15 percent", "Exactly 50 percent", "UPPP is 100 percent curative in all stages"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Stage III patients have massive tongues that completely block the view of the palate, and tiny tonsils. Doing a palatal UPPP does not address the main site of obstruction (the tongue base), resulting in an extremely high surgical failure rate.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Describe the "Friedman Tongue Position" (FTP) Class III (or modified Mallampati Class III) during oral cavity examination.', '["The tonsillar pillars and the entire uvula are completely visible", "The soft palate and the base of the uvula are visible, but the uvula tip and tonsillar pillars are obscured by the tongue base", "Only the hard palate and a tiny sliver of the soft palate are visible; the uvula and tonsils are completely hidden by the tongue base", "The hard palate is not even visible", "The tongue is completely paralyzed"]'::jsonb, 2, 'FTP Class III represents a crowded, "high-riding" tongue base. It strongly suggests that a major component of the patient''s sleep apnea is located lower down at the retrolingual (tongue base) level.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'The Brodsky Tonsil Grading scale is used to describe tonsillar hypertrophy. What defines a "Grade 3" tonsil?', '["Tonsils are confined to the tonsillar fossa", "Tonsils occupy less than 25 percent of the lateral airway dimension", "Tonsils occupy 50 to 75 percent of the lateral airway dimension, extending significantly beyond the tonsillar pillars", "Tonsils are touching each other in the midline (\"kissing tonsils\")", "Tonsils have been completely removed"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Brodsky grading is: Grade 1 (<25% airway obstruction), Grade 2 (25-50%), Grade 3 (50-75%), Grade 4 (>75%, "kissing"). Grade 3 and 4 tonsils are highly obstructive and indicate a very favorable outcome for tonsillectomy/UPPP.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the clinical significance of "Lingual Tonsil Hypertrophy" (LTH) in the pre-operative evaluation of an OSA patient?', '["It causes nasal congestion", "It is a rare benign tumor that should be ignored", "It represents hypertrophy of the lymphoid tissue at the base of the tongue, which is a major, often overlooked source of retrolingual airway obstruction", "It causes vocal cord paralysis", "It is only found in infants"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Lingual tonsils are part of Waldeyer''s ring, located on the tongue base. Hypertrophy (often as a compensatory response after prior palatine tonsillectomy) directly crowds the hypopharynx and requires advanced base-of-tongue surgical approaches.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'How is the "Müller Maneuver" performed during an awake clinic examination, and what is its major limitation?', '["The patient blows out against a closed nose; it has no limitations", "The patient inhales forcefully with their mouth and nose closed while a flexible endoscope is held in the pharynx; its major limitation is that it is performed awake and frequently overestimates palatal collapse while underestimating lateral wall collapse compared to true sleep", "It requires general anesthesia", "It measures lung capacity", "It is performed only while the patient is running on a treadmill"]'::jsonb, 1, 'While quick and easy, the Müller maneuver is a poor predictor of surgical outcomes because the active, awake muscle contraction does not accurately replicate the passive, atonic collapse that occurs during natural sleep.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the diagnostic procedure of choice for physically visualizing the exact dynamic sites and patterns of upper airway collapse during simulated sleep?', '["A static lateral neck X-ray", "Drug-Induced Sleep Endoscopy (DISE)", "A high-speed neck CT scan", "Awake fiberoptic laryngoscopy with Müller maneuver", "An MRI of the brain"]'::jsonb, 1, 'DISE has revolutionized modern sleep surgery. By pharmacologically sedating the patient in an operating room to mimic natural sleep, the surgeon can directly visualize the collapse of the soft palate, lateral walls, tongue base, and epiglottis in real time.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following medications is most commonly used by anesthesiologists during Drug-Induced Sleep Endoscopy (DISE) to achieve stable, titratable sedation without suppressing spontaneous respiration?', '["Ketamine alone", "Propofol (often via target-controlled infusion) or Dexmedetomidine", "Fentanyl and Midazolam in high doses", "Sevoflurane gas", "Succinylcholine"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Propofol and dexmedetomidine (a highly selective alpha-2 agonist) are ideal. Dexmedetomidine is particularly favored by some because it does not depress the respiratory drive and closely mimics natural slow-wave sleep physiology.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Why is the use of high-dose benzodiazepines or narcotics strictly avoided during Drug-Induced Sleep Endoscopy (DISE)?', '["They make the patient hyperactive", "They cause severe airway muscle relaxation and respiratory depression, which artificially induces severe collapse that does not reflect the patient''s natural sleep patterns", "They block the view of the endoscope", "They cause the patient to vomit", "They are too expensive"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The goal of DISE is to simulate the patient''s typical sleep airway. Over-sedating the patient with deep respiratory depressants creates a false, highly collapsed airway, leading to incorrect surgical decisions.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During DISE, the surgeon performs a "chin lift" or "jaw thrust" maneuver. What does a positive response (the airway instantly opening) to a jaw thrust suggest?', '["The patient requires a tracheostomy", "The patient is a highly favorable candidate for a Mandibular Advancement Device (MAD) or skeletal advancement surgery (like MMA)", "The palate is the sole site of obstruction", "The patient has central sleep apnea", "The anesthesia is too deep"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A jaw thrust mimics mandibular protrusion. If this mechanical action physically opens the collapsed retrolingual or retropalatal space during DISE, it strongly predicts that an oral appliance or surgical jaw advancement will successfully cure their OSA.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'The "VOTE" classification system is the standard method for reporting DISE findings. What does the "V" stand for?', '["Vocal folds", "Ventricle of the larynx", "Velum (soft palate, uvula, and lateral retro-velar walls)", "Vagus nerve", "Vallecula"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The VOTE system grades collapse at four anatomical levels: Velum (V), Oropharynx lateral walls (O), Tongue base (T), and Epiglottis (E).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'In the VOTE classification, what does the "O" stand for, and what specific tissues are collapsing?', '["Occipital region", "Oropharynx lateral walls, which includes the palatine tonsils and the lateral pharyngeal constrictor muscles", "Oral cavity", "Oesophagus", "Otitis media"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Lateral wall collapse (O) is a major cause of CPAP failure and is highly correlated with severe OSA. It requires specialized lateral pharyngoplasty techniques rather than standard midline UPPP.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A DISE report notes "Concentric collapse of the Velum." Why is this finding highly significant for an ENT surgeon planning a standard UPPP?', '["It indicates UPPP will be 100 percent successful", "Concentric collapse of the velum (where the lateral walls and posterior wall collapse inward simultaneously with the palate) is a strong predictor of standard UPPP failure, and is a strict contraindication for Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation", "It means the patient has central sleep apnea", "It requires immediate tracheostomy", "It is a normal finding"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Concentric collapse represents a global, circumferential sphincter-like narrowing. A standard UPPP (which only cuts the palate) will fail. Furthermore, Inspire therapy requires anteroposterior (AP) palatal collapse; concentric collapse is a strict, FDA-mandated exclusion criterion for hypoglossal nerve stimulation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During DISE, you observe the lateral pharyngeal walls collapsing inward, completely occluding the airway. What type of collapse pattern is this?', '["Anteroposterior (AP) collapse", "Lateral collapse", "Concentric collapse", "Vertical collapse", "Laryngeal spasm"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Lateral wall collapse is driven by bulk tissue in the lateral walls or weakness of the lateral constrictors. It requires surgeries that suspend or pull the lateral tissue outward, like an Expansion Sphincter Pharyngoplasty.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient undergoing DISE shows "Anteroposterior (AP) collapse of the Tongue Base." What does this mean anatomically?', '["The tongue is collapsing side-to-side", "The tongue base is falling directly backward against the posterior pharyngeal wall", "The tongue is swelling up", "The tongue is paralyzing the vocal cords", "The tongue is falling out of the mouth"]'::jsonb, 1, 'AP base of tongue collapse is the classic retrolingual obstruction pattern. It indicates the need for tongue-directed therapies (Inspire, MMA, midline glossectomy).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During DISE, you observe the epiglottis folding backward (retroflexing) like a trapdoor during every inspiration, completely blocking the glottis. What is this phenomenon called, and how is it surgically managed?', '["Epiglottitis; treated with IV antibiotics", "Floppy or trapdoor epiglottis; treated with a partial epiglottidectomy (removing the upper half of the epiglottis)", "Laryngomalacia; treated with UPPP", "Vocal cord paralysis; treated with Botox", "Tracheomalacia; treated with a stent"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A floppy epiglottis is an underdiagnosed cause of OSA. It is often pushed down by CPAP pressure, causing CPAP failure. Laser or cold-instrument resection of the floppy upper portion of the epiglottis (epiglottidectomy) is highly curative.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'In cephalometric analysis of the airway, what does the "MP-H" distance measure, and what does an ELEVATED value signify?', '["The distance from the maxillary plane to the hyoid bone; an elevated value means a short neck", "The distance from the Mandibular Plane to the Hyoid bone; an elevated value indicates a low-lying hyoid bone, which is strongly associated with increased airway collapsibility and severe OSA", "The size of the jaw", "The length of the palate", "The volume of the tongue"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The MP-H distance is a classic cephalometric predictor of OSA. A long MP-H (>20 mm) means the hyoid bone (and thus the base of the tongue) sits very low in the neck, stretching and destabilizing the pharyngeal airway, making it highly collapsible.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What cephalometric measurement is used to evaluate the position of the maxilla relative to the skull base, and what does a low angle indicate?', '["The SNA (Sella-Nasion-A point) angle; a low angle indicates maxillary hypoplasia/retroposition", "The SNB angle", "The ANB angle", "The MP-H distance", "The dental occlusion angle"]'::jsonb, 0, 'SNA evaluates the upper jaw. A normal SNA is ~82 degrees. A low SNA (<80 degrees) indicates a recessed upper jaw, which crowds the nasopharyngeal and retropalatal airway.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What cephalometric measurement is used to evaluate the position of the mandible (lower jaw) relative to the skull base, and what does a low angle indicate?', '["The SNA angle", "The SNB (Sella-Nasion-B point) angle; a low angle indicates mandibular retrognathia (recessed lower jaw)", "The ANB angle", "The posterior airway space", "The hyoid position"]'::jsonb, 1, 'SNB evaluates the lower jaw. A normal SNB is ~80 degrees. A low SNB (<78 degrees) confirms retrognathia, pushing the tongue base backward into the airway.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'The ANB angle represents the difference between the SNA and SNB angles. What does a highly positive or highly negative ANB angle tell the sleep surgeon?', '["The length of the nose", "The skeletal discrepancy between the upper jaw (maxilla) and lower jaw (mandible), which is critical for planning Maxillomandibular Advancement (MMA) surgery", "The volume of the tongue", "The risk of vocal cord paralysis", "The compliance of the CPAP machine"]'::jsonb, 1, 'ANB tells you how mismatched the jaws are. This is vital for skeletal surgery, ensuring that when the jaws are advanced, the patient''s dental bite (occlusion) can be reconstructed properly.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the "Posterior Airway Space" (PAS) on a lateral cephalometric radiograph, and what threshold is considered abnormally narrow?', '["The space in the nasal cavity", "The narrowest airway space behind the base of the tongue; a value less than 11 mm is considered narrow and highly suggestive of retrolingual obstruction", "The size of the trachea", "The distance between the vocal cords", "The size of the esophagus"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The PAS measures the retroglossal space. A PAS < 11 mm confirms that the base of the tongue is physically crowding the throat, making skeletal or tongue-directed surgery more likely to be needed.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following is considered a major limitation of standard awake, sitting cephalometric radiographs in evaluating an OSA patient?', '["They are too expensive", "They expose the patient to massive doses of radiation", "They are static, two-dimensional images taken while the patient is awake and upright, which does not capture the dynamic, three-dimensional airway collapse that occurs when the patient is supine and asleep", "They can only visualize bone, not soft tissue", "They are not reproducible"]'::jsonb, 2, 'While cephalometrics are excellent for diagnosing skeletal issues (retrognathia), they are static. An upright, awake airway is held open by muscle tone. It does not show where the airway actually collapses when the patient falls asleep on their back.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What does the presence of "Epiglottic lateral-to-lateral (mediolateral) collapse" during DISE suggest about the structural integrity of the epiglottis?', '["The epiglottis is too stiff", "It indicates severe laryngomalacia, where the lateral borders of the epiglottis curl inward (omega-shape) due to structurally weak cartilage", "The epiglottis is missing", "The epiglottis is normal", "The vocal cords are paralyzed"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Mediolateral collapse of the epiglottis is the adult equivalent of pediatric laryngomalacia, driven by cartilage floppiness rather than just a loose mucosal hinge.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Why is a detailed nasal examination (looking for septal deviation, turbinate hypertrophy, nasal polyps, or internal/external nasal valve collapse) a mandatory part of every OSA evaluation?', '["Because nasal obstruction causes central sleep apnea", "Because resolving nasal obstruction reduces downstream airway resistance and forces nasal breathing, which dramatically improves CPAP compliance and outcomes", "Because nasal surgery alone is a guaranteed cure for severe OSA", "Because nasal polyps always spread to the tongue", "To check the patient''s sense of smell"]'::jsonb, 1, 'While nasal surgery alone rarely cures OSA (it usually drops the AHI only slightly), it is incredibly important. Opening the nose reduces the "vacuum" effect in the throat, stops mouth-breathing jaw rotation, and makes wearing a CPAP mask comfortable.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient has an AHI of 50. During your exam, you notice massive bilateral grade 4 tonsils, a low-hanging soft palate, and a BMI of 25. What is the most appropriate surgical plan?', '["Hypoglossal nerve stimulation", "Maxillomandibular advancement (MMA)", "Uvulopalatopharyngoplasty (UPPP) with bilateral tonsillectomy; this patient has classic Friedman Stage I anatomy and is highly likely to be cured by soft tissue surgery", "Tracheostomy", "Nasal septoplasty alone"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This is the classic "home run" case for soft tissue surgery. The obstruction is dominated by huge tonsils and a long palate in a non-obese patient. Removing the tonsils and trimming the palate will wide open the airway.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which diagnostic imaging modality provides a highly detailed, three-dimensional reconstruction of the upper airway, allowing precise volumetric measurement of the airway and jaws before sleep surgery?', '["Standard lateral neck X-ray", "Cone Beam Computed Tomography (CBCT) or multi-slice airway CT scan", "Barium swallow", "Ultrasound of the neck", "Chest X-ray"]'::jsonb, 1, 'CBCT is widely used by sleep surgeons and maxillofacial surgeons. It provides low-dose, high-resolution 3D reconstructions to measure airway volume and plan precise skeletal cuts.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Why is an electrocardiogram (ECG/EKG) routinely reviewed or performed during the pre-operative workup for a patient with severe OSA scheduled for major sleep surgery?', '["To check for lung capacity", "To screen for underlying cardiac hypertrophy, right ventricular strain (pulmonary hypertension), or arrhythmias (like A-fib) triggered by chronic severe hypoxia, ensuring safe general anesthesia", "To measure the AHI", "To check for throat cancer", "To evaluate the jaw joint"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Severe OSA is a major cardiac stressor. Ensuring the patient''s heart can safely tolerate general anesthesia and the post-operative recovery period is a critical safety step.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with severe OSA is found to have a severe "retroposition" of the maxilla and mandible. Which surgical specialty is typically consulted to co-manage this patient''s skeletal discrepancy?', '["Neurosurgery", "Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery (OMFS)", "Cardiothoracic surgery", "Orthopedic surgery", "Neurology"]'::jsonb, 1, 'OMFS surgeons specialize in orthognathic surgery. They perform the bony cuts (osteotomies) needed for Maxillomandibular Advancement (MMA).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the role of "Virtual Surgical Planning" (VSP) in modern skeletal surgery for OSA?', '["It is a video game for the surgeon", "It utilizes 3D CT scans and advanced CAD/CAM software to pre-plan the exact bony cuts, the amount of jaw advancement, and to pre-fabricate custom titanium plates and templates, drastically increasing surgical precision and safety", "It replaces the actual surgery", "It measures CPAP compliance", "It diagnoses central sleep apnea"]'::jsonb, 1, 'VSP has revolutionized MMA. It allows the surgeon to simulate the entire surgery on a computer, custom-order the exact titanium plates, and ensure a perfect dental bite before the patient ever enters the OR.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During DISE, the surgeon notes that when the patient is placed in the lateral (side) position, their retrolingual collapse completely resolves. What does this suggest about their treatment options?', '["They must undergo a total glossectomy", "They are an excellent candidate for positional therapy (sleeping on their side) or a simple Mandibular Advancement Device, potentially avoiding major surgery", "They require a tracheostomy", "They have central sleep apnea", "They cannot tolerate CPAP"]'::jsonb, 1, 'DISE is excellent for identifying positional components. If lateral positioning clears the airway, conservative positional therapies are highly indicated.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following physical exam findings should immediately alert the sleep surgeon to a high risk of difficult intubation during general anesthesia?', '["A long neck", "A Mallampati Class IV airway, retrognathia, a short thick neck, and limited cervical spine extension", "Normal healthy teeth", "Grade 1 tonsils", "A high-pitched voice"]'::jsonb, 1, 'These are classic indicators of a "difficult airway." Sleep surgery patients are notoriously difficult to intubate because their airways are already crowded and prone to collapse.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the clinical definition of "Glossoptosis"?', '["A tumor of the tongue", "The posterior displacement of the tongue into the oral pharynx, which can cause severe airway obstruction", "The surgical removal of the tongue", "A hyperactive tongue reflex", "A long tongue"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Glossoptosis is the physical falling back of the tongue. It is a major pathophysiologic driver of retrolingual obstruction, especially in patients with small jaws.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient scheduled for sleep surgery has a history of severe daytime sleepiness and a BMI of 42. Why is their high BMI a critical factor in the pre-operative planning?', '["Obese patients require less anesthesia", "A high BMI (>35-40) significantly reduces the success rate of isolated soft tissue palatal surgery (UPPP), increases anesthetic risks, and often necessitates multi-level or skeletal surgery", "A high BMI prevents them from sleeping", "A high BMI causes central sleep apnea only", "A high BMI is a contraindication to all treatment"]'::jsonb, 1, 'In morbidly obese patients, the airway is crowded by global fat deposition. Trimming the palate alone (UPPP) rarely solves the problem, as lateral wall and retrolingual collapse persist.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During your pre-operative throat exam, you note a massive, elongated uvula that hangs down past the base of the tongue. What specific surgical procedure is designed to address this tissue?', '["Septoplasty", "Uvulopalatopharyngoplasty (UPPP) or uvulopalatal flap", "Midline glossectomy", "Tracheostomy", "Hypoglossal nerve stimulation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'An elongated uvula acts like a pendulum, vibrating (snoring) and physically blocking the retropalatal space. A UPPP or a tissue-preserving uvulopalatal flap reshapes and shortens this tissue.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is "Drug-Induced Sleep Endoscopy (DISE) target-controlled infusion (TCI)"?', '["A method of injecting steroids into the vocal cords", "The use of a computerized pump that delivers Propofol to maintain a precise, constant blood/brain concentration, ensuring the patient remains in a stable level of light sedation without waking up or stopping breathing", "A type of local anesthesia", "A test for central sleep apnea", "A way to measure snoring volume"]'::jsonb, 1, 'TCI pump technology allows the anesthesiologist to dial in the exact "target" concentration of propofol. This prevents the "rollercoaster" of sedation where the patient is alternately too awake or too deeply anesthetized.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'In the VOTE classification, what does a score of "2" represent for a specific anatomical subsite?', '["No collapse", "Partial collapse (airway narrows slightly)", "Complete collapse (the tissue fully occludes the airway lumen)", "The subsite cannot be visualized", "A normal finding"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The VOTE scoring is: 0 (no collapse, <50% narrowing), 1 (partial collapse, 50-75%), 2 (complete collapse, >75% to 100%). A score of 2 indicates a major site of obstruction.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'In the VOTE classification, what does the "E" stand for, and what are the two main collapse patterns observed here?', '["Esophagus", "Epiglottis; the collapse can be either Anteroposterior (folding backward) or Mediolateral (lateral walls curling inward)", "External ear", "Eustachian tube", "Ethmoid sinus"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The epiglottis (E) can collapse backward (AP) or inward from the sides (mediolateral), both of which require direct surgical correction (epiglottidectomy or supraglottoplasty).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the primary drawback of using "Dexmedetomidine" for DISE?', '["It causes severe respiratory depression", "It can cause significant bradycardia (slow heart rate) and hypotension, and it takes longer to set in and wear off compared to Propofol", "It causes hyperventilation", "It causes severe nausea", "It is not FDA approved"]'::jsonb, 1, 'While dexmedetomidine is excellent for preserving natural respiratory drive, the sleep surgeon must be prepared for its slower onset/offset and its potential to lower the heart rate and blood pressure.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A pre-operative sleep study shows the patient spends 90 percent of their sleep time in the supine position and has an AHI of 50. In the lateral position, their AHI drops to 5. What is the most appropriate initial, non-surgical recommendation?', '["Total laryngectomy", "Strict positional therapy (e.g., a bumper belt or lateral sleep device)", "Maxillomandibular advancement", "Tracheostomy", "No treatment is needed"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is a textbook case of strictly positional OSA. Before undergoing invasive surgery, the patient should try a lateral sleep device to see if they can tolerate sleeping on their side, which would resolve their severe OSA.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which cephalometric landmark represents the most anterior point of the frontonasal suture, used as a reference point for jaw angles?', '["Sella (S)", "Nasion (N)", "A-point (A)", "B-point (B)", "Menton (Me)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Nasion (N) is a critical craniofacial landmark. The angles SNA and SNB use the Nasion as the vertex to measure the forward projection of the upper and lower jaws.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which cephalometric landmark represents the center of the hypophyseal fossa (sella turcica), representing the cranial base?', '["Nasion (N)", "Sella (S)", "Gonion (Go)", "Pogonion (Pog)", "Menton (Me)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Sella (S) represents the posterior cranial base reference point. The line connecting Sella and Nasion (S-N line) represents the plane of the anterior cranial base.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What does the cephalometric landmark "A-point" (subspinale) represent?', '["The tip of the chin", "The deepest midline point on the anterior premaxillary contour between the anterior nasal spine and the incisor tooth (representing the maxilla)", "The jaw joint", "The hyoid bone", "The soft palate"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The A-point is the bony landmark used to define the anterior limit of the upper jaw (maxilla).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What does the cephalometric landmark "B-point" (supramentale) represent?', '["The deepest midline point on the anterior bony curvature of the mandible, between the alveolar crest and the chin (representing the mandible)", "The tip of the nose", "The base of the skull", "The hyoid bone", "The hard palate"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The B-point is the bony landmark used to define the anterior limit of the lower jaw (mandible).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the "Hyoid-to-Mandibular Plane" (H-MP) distance, and how does it correlate with OSA severity?', '["It measures tongue thickness", "It is the vertical distance from the hyoid bone to the mandibular plane; a longer distance (>15-20 mm) means the hyoid sits low in the neck, which is strongly correlated with increased OSA severity", "It measures jaw length", "It has no clinical significance", "It is only measured in children"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A low-riding hyoid bone (long H-MP) is a classic structural risk factor for OSA. It means the base of the tongue and the epiglottis are pulled down and back, narrowing the hypopharyngeal airway.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with severe retrognathia (SNB = 70 degrees) has severe OSA. Why is this patient a poor candidate for isolated nasal surgery?', '["Nasal surgery is too dangerous", "Their obstruction is primarily skeletal and located at the retrolingual (tongue base) level due to their tiny jaw; opening the nose will not address the retropositioned tongue base", "Nasal surgery causes weight gain", "Retrognathia causes the nose to swell", "They don''t have a nose"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Nasal surgery is supportive but not corrective for skeletal airway crowding. The surgeon must explain that nasal surgery will help them breathe through their nose, but they will still need a CPAP or skeletal surgery (MMA) to cure their sleep apnea.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During DISE, the patient snoring is noted to be extremely loud, but the airway remains wide open with zero collapse. What is the diagnosis?', '["Severe OSA", "Primary Snoring (without obstructive sleep apnea)", "Central sleep apnea", "Vocal cord paralysis", "Laryngeal cancer"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Primary snoring represents tissue vibration without significant airway obstruction or oxygen desaturation. While socially disruptive, it does not carry the cardiovascular risks of true OSA.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which pre-operative screening tool is designed to identify patients at a high risk for difficult mask ventilation during general anesthesia?', '["The Epworth Sleepiness Scale", "The Han classification (combined with risk factors like age, BMI, beard, lack of teeth, history of snoring)", "The Cotton-Myer grade", "The House-Brackmann scale", "The Centor criteria"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Anesthesiologists evaluate for difficult mask ventilation (DMV). OSA patients are at a highly elevated risk for DMV due to their collapsible, bulky upper airway tissues.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Why is a history of severe sleep apnea critical for the post-operative recovery team (PACU nurses) to know?', '["Because the patient will wake up aggressive", "Because these patients are at an extremely high risk for post-obstructive pulmonary edema (POPE) or sudden airway collapse when sedated with post-op opioids", "Because they will require a private room", "Because they snore loudly", "It is not important"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Post-op monitoring is a critical safety issue. The combination of narcotics, residual anesthesia, and supine positioning can lead to fatal airway obstruction or negative-pressure pulmonary edema in the recovery room.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient undergoing DISE is noted to have complete, concentric collapse of the velum. Why does this finding rule them out for Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation (Inspire) therapy?', '["Because the tongue is not involved", "Inspire therapy works by pulling the tongue and soft palate forward (anteroposteriorly); it cannot open a circumferentially collapsing (concentric) velar sphincter. The FDA strictly excludes these patients", "Because concentric collapse cannot be treated with surgery", "Because the patient has central sleep apnea", "Because the machine is too large for their neck"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is a high-yield clinical fact. A concentric collapse pattern at the velum during DISE is a absolute contraindication for hypoglossal nerve stimulation. These patients are instead steered toward pharyngoplasty or MMA.')
